**Preparation (diazotisation):** aniline + NaNO₂ + HCl at 0–5 °C → **benzenediazonium chloride** (unstable above 5 °C, so kept cold).

**Synthetic uses:**
- with CuCN/KCN → benzonitrile (Sandmeyer);
- with CuCl/HCl → chlorobenzene;
- with H₃PO₂ or ethanol → benzene;
- with phenol in mild alkali → an **azo dye** (p-hydroxyazobenzene), the coupling reaction that makes dyes;
- hydrolysis by warm water → phenol.

Extraction involves concentration of the ore, conversion to the oxide, reduction to the metal and finally refining.

**Reduction choices follow the reactivity series:**
- the most reactive metals (Na, K, Ca, Al) are obtained by **electrolysis** of their molten salts or oxides (Al by Hall–Héroult);
- metals of medium reactivity (Fe, Zn, Cu) are reduced by **carbon or CO** in the blast furnace — Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂;
- less reactive and sulphide/special ores use self-reduction or **displacement** — e.g. Cu₂S with the oxide, or Cr₂O₃ reduced by Al (thermite, ΔH very negative);
- the most unreactive metals occur free (Au, Ag) or are refined by electrolysis after cyanide extraction.

All three are **electrophilic substitution** on the aromatic ring, though the ring's delocalised π electrons are preserved:

**(a)** nitration — the nitronium ion NO₂⁺ attacks, giving nitrobenzene;

**(b)** chlorination — the Cl⁺/FeCl₄⁻ complex gives chlorobenzene;

**(c)** Friedel–Crafts alkylation — the methyl cation gives toluene (with polyalkylation and rearrangement as side reactions).

Mechanism: electrophile attacks → arenium (σ-complex) intermediate → loss of H⁺ restores aromaticity.
